## “It works on my machine”

Everyone who has ever moved a program to another computer has this story. On the laptop the site works, and on the server it doesn’t: the language version is different, a library is missing, the web server is set up another way. People used to close that gap with ten pages of instructions (“install this, then that, then fix that file”), and the instructions went stale.

The solution was borrowed from shipping. Cargo used to travel any which way: sacks, barrels, crates, and every port loaded it differently. The standard **container** changed everything: whatever is inside, the port, the train and the truck treat the box the same. In programming the logic is the same. A program is packed together with everything it needs, and from then on you don’t care which computer runs it.

## Image, container, volume

These are three words you need to keep apart.

- An **image** is a ready, unchangeable “box”: a trimmed-down system, your program, its libraries and settings. An image is made of **layers**, each adding something to the one before.
- A **container** is a running instance of an image: a process that believes it has a file system and a network of its own. You can run as many containers as you like from one image. An image is the recipe for a cake, and a container is the cake on the table.
- A **volume** is a place for data that lives apart from the container. Whatever a container wrote inside itself disappears with it. So what you’d hate to lose (requests, uploaded files, the database) is kept on a volume or in a separate database.

There is also the **registry**: a store of images, where they are downloaded from (Docker Hub, and the big clouds and GitHub have registries of their own). From a registry you take other people’s images (a ready-made nginx, say), and into a registry you publish your own.

You’ll remember the difference from a **virtual machine** in one phrase: a virtual machine carries a whole operating system with it, while a container uses the host system’s kernel and isolates only its own process. So a container takes megabytes and starts in seconds. The price: the isolation is weaker than a virtual machine’s.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what happens to the data a container wrote inside itself if you remove it?</summary>

It disappears with the container. For data that has to be kept you need a volume or a separate database.

</details>

## The recipe for an image

An image is described in a file named `Dockerfile`: short lines of instructions, each making one layer.

```text
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY dist/ /usr/share/nginx/html/
EXPOSE 80
```

Read it as a recipe. `FROM` says which ready image to start from (here the nginx web server in the light alpine build). `COPY` puts your site’s built files from the `dist` folder into the image (the build gives you that folder, as you learned in the parts on JavaScript and generators). `EXPOSE` warns that the thing inside listens on port 80. Everything else needed to serve files is already in nginx.

When the site has to be built first, a **multi-stage build** helps: in the first stage Node.js builds the site, and in the second only the finished result goes into a clean nginx, with no Node and no sources.

```text
FROM node:lts-alpine AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY package.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html
EXPOSE 80
```

Two features save time and nerves. First: Docker remembers layers, and if an instruction and everything before it are unchanged, it takes the layer from the cache. So you copy what rarely changes first (`package.json`) and what changes often after it (the code). Second, and the main one: **secrets don’t go into an image**. The line `ENV API_KEY=...` writes the key into a layer for good: images get published and copied, and the key travels with them. Secrets are passed at launch. A small thing: a `.dockerignore` file keeps what you don’t need (a `.env`, say, or the `node_modules` folder) out of the image.

## Several containers as one project

A project usually has several parts: the site, a program that receives requests, sometimes a database. Starting each with a command of five flags is awkward, so there is **Compose**: one file, `compose.yaml`, describes all the containers, ports, volumes and the links between them.

```yaml
services:
  site:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    restart: unless-stopped
  receiver:
    build: ./receiver
    env_file: .env
    volumes:
      - requests:/data
    restart: unless-stopped
volumes:
  requests:
```

Here `ports: "8080:80"` joins port 8080 of your computer to port 80 inside the container (the left is “outside”, the right “inside”). `env_file: .env` takes secrets from a file that goes neither into the image nor into the repository. `requests:/data` attaches the volume `requests` to the receiver’s folder `/data`, and the volume is declared in the `volumes` section at the bottom. To start the whole project: `docker compose up -d`; its state: `docker compose ps`; to stop it: `docker compose down` (the volumes stay).

## Try it in the simulator

There is no real Docker in a browser, so this is a **simulator**: the page reads the commands itself. But the order of things and the messages are real. Choose a Dockerfile (in the third one a key is written into the image, so see what the build says), build the image, run the container, open the site with `curl`, then stop and remove the container.

<DockerSim />

What to notice. If you run a container without `-p`, it works but can’t be opened from outside. If a port is already taken by another container, you get an error. A second build takes unchanged layers from the cache. And the site `curl` returns is signed with your project’s name.

If you want to try it for real, install Docker Desktop or its free replacement Podman and repeat the same commands in a terminal. Docker Desktop is free for individuals, for education, for non-commercial open source, and for companies with fewer than 250 employees and less than $10 million a year in revenue; larger organizations need a paid subscription (Docker’s FAQ, October 2026). Docker Engine itself on Linux is open source.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what does -p 8080:80 mean in the run command?</summary>

Port 8080 of your computer is joined to port 80 inside the container. Without it the container works but can’t be opened from outside.

</details>

## Honestly: when Docker is overkill

Containers have a price: you have to learn them, update the base images (vulnerabilities are found in those too), and watch the disk and the logs. So the honest question is: what do they solve for you?

- **A site of static files.** You don’t need Docker at all: the files go on static hosting, which is cheaper and more reliable. A container with nginx for one page is a complicated way of doing something simple.
- **One machine, a fixed environment.** An “identical box” gains nothing.
- **Several programs that must run the same way** for you, your colleague and the server (the site, a request receiver, a database): here Docker and Compose pay off.
- **A project whose main action a ready-made service covers** (receiving forms, booking, payment): there is nothing to pack.

Compare the first lesson of the part. The steps of hosting were a ladder too, and Docker is one more rung, not a necessity.

## Practice

In front of you is your project’s site, and under it two files in boxes: a Dockerfile and compose.yaml. Finish them on the model in the lesson. Then open the simulator, build the image, run the container with its port, and paste the line from `docker ps` into the note. Say what is on the volume (through your project’s main action), and answer the last question: do you need Docker?

The check reads the texts in the boxes. Docker can’t be run in the sandbox, so running it in the simulator remains your job, and the note shows that it worked.

## What was wrong before

In 2010 the unit of moving was a server: an admin set up a machine by hand, and a year later no one remembered how exactly. Such servers were called “snowflakes”: each is unique, and you can’t make a copy. A move meant a week of manual work and several “oh, we also need to install…”. A container turned the setup into a file that lies in the repository, can be read and reproduced. But, as always with convenient things, the main point remains: a container packs not the knowledge but only its record, and understanding what is inside is still your job.
